# Keep the build context to what the image actually needs.
#
# Before this existed, `COPY backend/ ./` was dragging in backend/.venv (4,538
# files, 101 MB) and __pycache__ on every build: a 173 MB context for an image
# whose real content is a few megabytes.
#
# The `**/` prefixes are load-bearing. A bare `.venv` matches only `./.venv`; the
# virtualenv here is at `backend/.venv`, so without `**/` it is silently copied
# in and the image ships a 100 MB directory of Windows-built wheels that nothing
# can import. A `.dockerignore` that looks right and matches nothing is worse
# than none, because it reads as if the problem is handled.

**/.venv/
**/venv/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/*.egg-info/

**/node_modules/
frontend/dist/

.git/
.github/
.vscode/
.idea/
**/*.log

# Local scratch and verification artifacts.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# NOTE on the database.
#
# Excluded on purpose. The image ships the raw OSM snapshot in `backend/data/`
# and the entrypoint builds the city from it on first boot, so a database is a
# build output rather than an input.
#
# This was previously NOT excluded, which was a bug with two faces. It shipped a
# local database into every image, and that database had been created by
# `Base.metadata.create_all` rather than by migrations -- so the entrypoint's
# `alembic upgrade head` hit "table restaurants already exists" and the container
# died on boot. It appeared to work only because a pre-existing database already
# carried `alembic_version` at head.
#
# `backend/data/` is deliberately kept: 348 KB of gzipped OSM payload plus a few
# hundred bytes of provenance, and it is the source the city is built from.
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
